  issn = {07294360 (ISSN)},
  doi = {10.1080/0729436920110202},
  abstract = {The purpose of this study was to identify the writing errors made by 310 first year university students in 13 disciplines using a checklist of five writing error categories, each with a number of sub-categories. The overall median error rate was 35.0 errors per 1000 words. Punctuation and capitalisation was by far the most common category of error, and sentence structure, word usage, spelling and vocabulary followed in descending order of frequency. Law and Economics students exhibited the highest error rates while Geography, Mechanical Engineering, Philosophy, English, Statistics, Linguistics, Geology, History and Sociology students had error rates near the median value, and French and Psychology students made fewest errors. The major error sub-categories that best indicated difference between good and poor writers were the use of commas and some aspects of sentence construction. Copyright \copyright{} 1992, Taylor \& Francis Group, LLC. All rights reserved.},
