ry of learning, innovation and change (2009) Educational Research Review, 4 (1), pp. 16-25. , doi:10.1016/j.edurev.2008.06.001; Torres-Velasquez, D., Sociocultural theory: Standing at the crossroads (2000) Remedial and Special Education, 21 (2), pp. 66-69. , doi:10.1177/074193250002100201; Vagan, A., Towards a sociocultural perspective on identity formation in education (2011) Mind, Culture and Activity, 18 (1), pp. 43-57. , doi:10.1080/10749031003605839; Williams, B., The theoretical links between problem-based learning and self-directed learning for continuing professional nursing education (2001) Teaching in Higher Education, 6 (1), pp. 85-98. , doi:10.1080/13562510020029626; Wortham, S., (2001) Narratives in action. A strategy for research and analysis, , New York, NY, New York, NY,: Teachers College Press; Yan, M.C., Exploring cultural tensions in cross-cultural social work practice (2008) Social Work, 53 (4), pp. 317-328. , doi:10.1093/sw/53.4.317},
  abstract = {It can sometimes be difficult to engage students in 'real life experiences' within the classroom. In one Bachelor of Social Work program, the development of a Social Work Studio (the Studio) has provided students with opportunities to engage in simulated social work practice in a safe and supportive environment. This article reports on a small exploratory study of students' responses to a survey that sought their views and reactions to the development of this Studio initiative. Findings were analysed from a sociocultural perspective. They indicate that students' responses to the development of the Studio reflect their emerging constructions of the cultural world of social work. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {simulation,skills development,social work practice,sociocultural perspectives of learning,student engagement}
}

