ltural learning space (2009) Journal of Studies in International Education, 13 (2), pp. 240-255. , doi:10.1177/1028315308329789; (2006) The experiences of international students in UK further education colleges: New Horizons - Report of the UKCOSA survey, , http://www.ukcosa.org.uk/files/pdf/new_horizons_report.pdf, UKCOSA, London, London,: UKCOSA - The Council for International Education, Retrieved January 10, 2011, from; Volet, S., Ang, G., Culturally mixed groups on international campuses: An opportunity for co-operative learning (1998) Higher Education Research and Development, 17 (1), pp. 5-23. , doi:10.1080/0729436980170101; Ward, C., Masgoret, A., (2004) The experiences of international students in New Zealand. Report on the results of national survey, , http://www.educationcounts.govt.nz/publications/international/14700, New Zealand Ministry of Education. Retrieved January 10, 2011, from; Wong, J.K., Are learning styles of Asian international students culturally or contextually based? (2004) International Education Journal, 4 (4), pp. 154-166; Wright, S., Lander, D., Collaborative group interactions of students from two ethnic backgrounds (2003) Higher Education Research and Development, 22 (3), pp. 237-251. , doi:10.1080/0729436032000145121},
  abstract = {This article explores the use of group work strategies to increase student interaction and learning. Despite the growing linguistic and cultural diversity in tertiary institutions, there is strong evidence of minimal interaction between 'domestic' and 'international' students in classrooms and in wider university contexts. This study investigates the implementation of teaching and learning strategies in an undergraduate class comprising domestic and international students from Education and Arts. The strategies relate to in-class group work, tutorial groups and assessment design. The findings indicate greater class interaction, higher satisfaction ratings and better learning outcomes as a result of the strategies. The article argues for three key features underpinning the pedagogy: where international students can work from a position of power equality in class, where both groups of students can enact the role of 'experts' and where support in language and learning how to learn is embedded in assessment and outcomes. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
