  abstract = {Vote Gun: How Gun Rights Became Politicized in the United States by Patrick J. Charles offers a revisionist history of the National Rifle Association (NRA), challenging the conventional narrative that the organization only became politicized after the 1960s. Drawing on archival sources, NRA publications, and political history, Charles argues that the NRA has long been a politically active entity, expertly navigating insider status within government while cultivating a grassroots base through identity politics and ideological indoctrination. The book demonstrates that the NRA's image as a moderate, service-oriented organization was a strategic fa \& ccedil;ade that masked decades of coordinated resistance to firearms regulation. Charles situates the transformation of the NRA within broader societal and political shifts, particularly the rise in crime, political violence, and the advent of organized opposition in the 1960s. His work reframes the gun debate as a long-standing struggle over American identity, civic virtue, and the boundaries of government authority.},
