, doi:10.1080/00091380309604090; Le, B., Agnew, C.R., Commitment and its theorized determinants: A meta-analysis of the Investment Model (2003) Personal Relationships, 10 (1), pp. 37-57. , doi:10.1111/1475-6811.00035; Lieberman, M.S., (1998) Commitment, value and moral realism, , Cambridge, Cambridge,: Cambridge University Press; Lucas, R.E., Donnellan, M.B., How stable is happiness? Using the STARTS model to estimate the stability of life satisfaction (2007) Journal of Research in Personality, 41 (5), pp. 1091-1098. , doi:10.1016/j.jrp.2006.11.005; Madsen, S.R., Turnbull, O., Academic service learning experiences of compensation and benefit course students (2006) Journal of Management Education, 30 (5), pp. 724-742. , doi:10.1177/1052562905283710; Matthews, K.E., Andrews, V., Adams, P., Social learning spaces and student engagement (2010) Higher Education Research and Development, 30 (2), pp. 105-120; Moely, B.E., McFarland, M., Miron, D., Mercer, S., Ilustre, V., Changes in college students' attitudes and intentions for civic involvement as a function of service-learning experiences (2002) Michigan Journal of Community Service Learning, Fall, pp. 18-26; Morosanu, L., Handley, K., O'Donovan, B., Seeking support: Researching first-year students' experiences of coping with academic life (2010) Higher Education Research and Development, 29 (6), pp. 665-678. , doi:10.1080/07294360.2010.487200; Power, A., Community engagement as authentic learning with reflection (2009) Issues in Educational Research, 20 (1), pp. 57-63; Rusbult, C.E., Martz, J.M., Agnew, C.R., The Investment Model Scale: Measuring commitment level, satisfaction level, quality of alternatives and investment size (1998) Personal Relationships, 5 (4), pp. 357-391. , doi:10.1111/j.1475-6811.1998.tb00177.x; Smith, B.H., Gahagan, J., McQuillin, S., Haywood, B., Cole, C.P., Bolton, C., Wampler, M.K., The development of a service-learning program for first-year students based on the hallmarks of high quality service-learning and rigorous program evaluation (2011) Innovative Higher Education, 36 (5), pp. 317-329. , doi: 10.1007/s10755-011-9177-9; Steger, M.F., Kashdan, T.B., Stability and specificity of meaning in life and life satisfaction over one year (2007) Journal of Happiness Studies, 8 (2), pp. 161-179. , doi:10.1007/s10902-006-9011-8; Stupans, I., Scutter, S., Pearce, K., Facilitating student learning: Engagement in novel learning opportunities (2010) Innovative Higher Education, 35 (5), pp. 359-366. , doi:10.1007/s10755-010-9148-6; Westheimer, J., Kahne, J., What kind of citizen? The politics of educating for democracy (2004) American Educational Research Journal, 41 (2), pp. 237-269. , doi:10.3102/00028312041002237; Willcoxson, L., Factors affecting intention to leave in the first, second and third year of university studies: A semester-by-semester investigation (2011) Higher Education Research and Development, 29 (6), pp. 623-639},
  abstract = {This preliminary study examines the utility of the Community Engagement Commitment Scale and reports on factors that predict student-teachers' commitment to community engagement in their second year of study at a South African university. Independent variables included Dalbert's personal belief in a just world, an adaptation of Rusbult et al.'s original predictors of Commitment level (Satisfaction, Quality of alternatives, Investment size) and a new factor, Meaningfulness. The latter factor is based theoretically on the work of Lieberman and Heine, Proulx and Vohs and was included to study the extent to which identity-expression could be related to Commitment level. We administered the Community Engagement Commitment Scale on a non-random sample of 414 student-teachers in their second year of study, who all participated in compulsory community engagement project. The results support a four-factor model of Commitment with Meaningfulness, Quality of alternatives and Investment as predictors of Commitment level. Surprisingly, all the Satisfaction items loaded with the Commitment level items on one factor. Together, Meaningfulness, Quality of alternatives and Investment accounted for approximately two-thirds of the variance in Commitment level. Significant (p \&.01) language group differences were also observed on Meaningfulness and Commitment level. The results of the study suggest the importance of studying meaningfulness as an additional predictor of Commitment level in community engagement contexts. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
