utcomes and higher order thinking skills Proceedings of the ATN Evaluations and Assessment Conference, , Brisbane, November 29-30; Oliver, B., (2011) Assuring graduate capabilities: Good practice report, , http://www.altc.edu.au/resource-assuring-graduate-outcomes-curtin-2011, Sydney. Retrieved August 6, 2011, from; Robinson, D., The risk recreation experience: Subjective state dimensions and the transferability of benefits (1992) Journal of Applied Recreation Research, 17 (1), pp. 12-36; Smith, E., Green, A., (2005) How workplace experiences while at school affect career pathways, , Adelaide, Adelaide,: National Centre for Vocational and Educational Research; Starkey, K., Tempest, S., The winter of our discontent: The design challenge for business schools (2009) Academy of Management, Learning and Education, 8 (4), pp. 576-586. , doi:10.5465/AMLE.2009.47785476; Tennant, M., Is learning transferable? (1999) Understanding learning at work, pp. 165-179. , In: Boud D., Garrick J., editors London, London,: Routledge; Thorndike, E., Woodworth, R., The influence of improvement in one mental function upon the efficiency of other functions (1901) Psychological Review, 8, pp. 247-261. , doi:10.1037/h0074898; Wheeler, J., The impact of social environments on emotional, social and cognitive development (2008) Journal of Management Development, 27 (1), pp. 129-145. , doi:10.1108/02621710810840802; Whitelaw, P., (2010) Re-conceptualising hospitality management: Analysing and predicting career progression and success in hospitality, , (PhD thesis, Victoria University); Wilton, N., Business graduates and management jobs: An employability match made in heaven? (2008) Journal of Education for Work, 21 (2), pp. 143-158. , doi:10.1080/13639080802080949; Wilton, N., Do employability skills really matter in the UK graduate labour market? The case of business and management graduates (2011) Work, Employment and Society, 25 (1), pp. 85-100. , doi:10.1177/0950017010389244; Wittekind, A., Raeder, S., Grote, G., A longitudinal study of determinants of perceived employability (2010) Journal of Organizational Behavior, 31 (4), pp. 566-586; Wolfe, C., Reyna, V., Brainerd, C., Fuzzy-Trace Theory: Implications in teaching and learning (2005) Transfer of learning from a modern multidisciplinary perspective, , In: Mestre J., editors Greenwich, CT, Greenwich, CT,: Information Age; Yashin-Shaw, I., Buckridge, M., Buckridge, P., Ferres, K., Enhancing graduate attributes: Mechanisms for facilitating the transfer of academically-acquired generic skills into the workplace Proceedings of the 3rd Annual Lifelong Learning Conference, , Yeppoon, Queensland, June; Yorke, M., Knight, P., (2004) Embedding employability into the curriculum, , York, York,: Higher Education Academy},
  abstract = {Persistent gaps in certain non-technical skills in business graduates continue to impact on organisational performance and global competitiveness. Despite business schools' best efforts in developing non-technical skills, widely acknowledged as fundamental to graduate employability, there has been considerably less attention to measuring skill outcomes and even less on their subsequent transfer to the workplace. It appears stakeholders are assuming transfer occurs automatically in graduates, neglecting the influence of learning program, learner and workplace characteristics on this complex process and its potential impact on graduate employability. This paper unpacks the concept of transfer and proposes a model of graduate employability that incorporates the process. Measures for empirical analysis are discussed. Testing the model would indicate the extent to which transfer occurs and highlight collaborative strategies for employers, universities and graduates in nurturing learning and workplace environments in which transfer may flourish, taking us one step closer to the elusive 'work-ready' graduate. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {business,employability,generic skills,graduate attributes,transfer}
}

