earch and Development, 28 (3), pp. 303-318. , doi:10.1080/07294360902839909; Elton, L., Strategies to enhance student motivation: A conceptual analysis (1996) Studies in Higher Education, 21 (1), pp. 57-68. , doi:10.1080/03075079612331381457; Hanson, J.M., Sinclair, K.E., Social constructivist teaching methods in Australian universities - reported uptake and perceived learning effects: A survey of lecturers (2008) Higher Education Research & Development, 27 (3), pp. 169-186. , doi:10.1080/07294360802183754; Harris, J., Mishra, P., Koehler, M., Teachers' technological pedagogical content knowledge and learning activity types: Curriculum-based technology integration reframed (2009) Journal of Research on Technology in Education, 41 (4), pp. 393-416; Harris, L., (2006) Redefining the teaching space as public: An essential step in implementing lasting change in Australian universities, , http://www.auqa.edu.au/qualityenhancement/publications/occasional/, Retrieved September 29, 2009, from publications/index.shtml; Haslett, S., Unpicking the links between research and teaching in higher education. University of Wales, Newport (2009) Newport CELT Journal, 2, pp. 1-4; Havnes, A., Peer-mediated learning beyond the curriculum (2008) Studies in Higher Education, 33 (2), pp. 193-204. , doi:10.1080/03075070801916344; Hedberg, J.G., Harper, B.M., Brown, C., Corderoy, R., Exploring user interfaces to improve learner outcomes (1994) Interactive multimedia in university education: Designing for change in teaching, pp. 15-29. , In: Beatie K., McNaught C., Wills S., editors Amsterdam, Holland, Amsterdam,: Elsevier; Hedberg, J.G., E-learning futures? Speculations for a time yet to come (2006) Studies in Continuing Education, 28 (2), pp. 173-185. , doi:10.1080/01580370600751187; Hedberg, J.G., Brudvik, O.C., Supporting dialogic literacy through mashing and modding of places and spaces (2008) Theory into Practice, 47 (2), pp. 138-149. , doi:10.1080/00405840801992363; Kenney, J.L., (2012) Using learning technologies to teach consumer behaviour, , Unpublished PhD thesis, Macquarie University, Sydney, Australia; Liebowitz, B., What's inside the suitcase? An investigation into the powerful resources students and lecturers bring to teaching and learning (2009) Higher Education Research & Development, 28 (3), pp. 261-274. , doi:10.1080/07294360902839875; McDonald, R.J., Professional development for information communication technology integration: Identifying and supporting a community of practice through design-based research (2008) Journal of Research on Technology in Education, 40 (4), pp. 429-445; Merritt, D.J., Bias, the rain, and student evaluations of teaching (2008) St John's Law Review, 82 (1), pp. 235-287; Reeves, T.C., Can educational research be both rigorous and relevant? (2011) Educational Designer, 1 (4), pp. 1-24; Ruthven, K., Laborde, C., Leach, J., Tiberghien, A., Tools in didactic research: Instrumenting the epistemological and cognitive aspects of the design of teaching sequences (2009) Educational Researcher, 38 (5), pp. 329-492. , doi:10.3102/0013189X09338513; Sandoval, W., Developing learning theory by refining conjectures embodied in educational designs (2004) Educational Psychologist, 39 (4), pp. 213-223. , doi:10.1207/s15326985ep3904_3; Scott, G., (2008) University student engagement and satisfaction with learning and teaching, , http://www.altc.edu.au/resource-university-student-engagement-scott-uws-2008, Retrieved October 19, 2009, from; Sharpe, R., Benfield, G., Roberts, G., Francis, R., (2006) The undergraduate experience of blended e-learning: A review of UK literature and practice, , http://www.heacademy.ac.uk/assets/York/documents/ourwork/research/literature_reviews/blended_elearning_full_review.pdf, Retrieved January 18, 2010, from; Sloane, F.C., Gorard, S., Exploring modeling aspects of design experiments (2003) Educational Researcher, 32 (1), pp. 29-31. , doi:10.3102/0013189X032001029; Susman, G., (1983) Action research: A sociotechnical systems perspective, , In: Morgan G., editors London, London,: Sage},
  abstract = {This paper presents an example of constructive alignment in practice. Integrated technology supports were deployed to increase the consistency between learning objectives, activities and assessment and to foster student-centred, higher-order learning processes in the unit. Modifications took place over nine iterations of a second-year Marketing unit and were guided by a design-based research approach. The aligned technologies were embedded in voluntary weekly tasks, feedback, lecture participation and research skill acquisition. Findings indicate that constructive alignment strengthens and is strengthened by technology supports, resulting in consistently improved learner experiences in small (under 60) and large (over 500) face-to-face cohorts, over time and in comparison to benchmark data derived from over 65 similar units. Heuristic themes emerging from the study conceive constructively aligned technology interventions as gateways, springboards and enablers to improved learning and learner experiences. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {design-based research,large cohorts,online support for face-to-face classes,student experience,technology-supported learning}
}

