archer in doctoral education (2008) Journal of Higher Education, 79 (3), pp. 326-350. , doi:10.1353/jhe.0.0007; Hammersley, M., Atkinson, P., (2007) Ethnography: Principles in practice, , 3, London, London,: Routledge; Harper, D., What's new visually? (2005) In N. Denzin & Y. Lincoln (Eds.), The Sage handbook of qualitative research, pp. 747-762. , 3, Thousand Oaks, CA, Thousand Oaks, CA,: Sage; Henkel, M., (2000) Academic identities and policy change in higher education, , London, London,: Jessica Kingsley; Kandlbinder, P., Recognition and influence: The evolution of higher education research and development (2012) Higher Education Research & Development, 31 (1), pp. 5-13. , doi:10.1080/07294360.2012.642836; King, V., Life classes: Revealing the influence of art school pedagogy on eLearning (2005) In Designs on eLearning Proceedings, , [CD-ROM] (pp. 1?3). September 14-16, 2005. London: University of the Arts; King, V., Novel readings: The history of a writing community by a partial, prejudiced and ignorant historian (2012) Arts and Humanities in Higher Education, , doi:10.1177/1474022212467587; Klein, J., (2009) Grayson Perry, , London, London,: Thames & Hudson; Lakoff, G., Johnson, M., (1980) Metaphors we live by, , Chicago, IL, Chicago, IL,: The University of Chicago Press; Land, R., Meyer, J.H.F., Smith, J., (2008) Threshold concepts within the disciplines, , In: Land R., Meyer J. H.F., Smith J., editors Rotterdam, Rotterdam,: Sense; Macfarlane, B., The higher education research archipelago (2012) Higher Education Research & Development, 31 (1), pp. 129-131. , doi:10.1080/07294360.2012.642846; Meyer, J.H.F., Land, R., Threshold concepts and troublesome knowledge (2): Epistemological considerations and a conceptual framework for teaching and learning (2005) Higher Education, 49 (3), pp. 373-388. , doi:10.1007/s10734-004-6779-5; Monmonier, M., (1991) How to lie with maps, , Chicago, IL, Chicago, IL,: University of Chicago Press; Morris, D., Ecclesfield, N., A new computer-aided technique for qualitative document analysis (2011) International Journal of Research & Method in Education, 34 (3), pp. 241-254. , doi:10.1080/1743727X.2011.609547; Oancea, A., Criticisms of educational research: Key topics and levels of analysis (2005) British Educational Research Journal, 31 (2), pp. 157-183. , doi:10.1080/0141192052000340198; Sinclair, S., Rockwell, G., (2012) Cirrus, , http://voyeurtools.org/tool/Cirrus/, Retrieved April 4, 2012, from; Smith, J., Forging identities: The experiences of probationary lecturers in the UK (2010) Studies in Higher Education, 35 (5), pp. 577-591. , doi:10.1080/03075070903216650; Tight, M., (2012) Researching higher education, , 2, Maidenhead, UK, Maidenhead, UK,: Open University Press/McGraw-Hill Education; Trowler, P., Wicked issues in situating theory in close-up research (2012) Higher Education Research & Development, 31 (3), pp. 273-284. , doi:10.1080/07294360.2011.631515; Wenger, E., (1998) Communities of practice: Learning, meaning and identity, , Cambridge, Cambridge,: Cambridge University Press},
  abstract = {This paper introduces the 'map', the 'novel' and the 'grid', three strategies inspired by the creative arts that potentially enrich higher education research by revealing layers of truth otherwise difficult to discern. The 'map' was inspired by the Map of an Englishman, an etching by Grayson Perry RA. It is used to create and analyse a visualisation of academic identity. The 'novel' depends upon finding an appropriate work of fiction to provide an analytical framework of metaphorical themes which help to draw out social and cultural issues. The 'grid' visually encodes the themes underlying large quantities of electronic communication to create a graphical pr\'ecis of discourse at a chosen point in time. I demonstrate these strategies in an autoethnographic study of academic identity. My findings echo and enhance those of other studies into academic identity, suggesting the strategies could be helpful ways of seeing and understanding the research context. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
