  issn = {07294360 (ISSN)},
  doi = {10.1080/07294360.2013.864613 10.1080/03075079.2012.754858; Caesar, T., The specter of collegiality (2007) Symploke, 13 (1-2), pp. 7-17; Cassidy, B., Hierarchy and collegiality in Australian universities (1998) Australian Universities' Review, 41 (2), pp. 43-48; Cederström, C., Hoedemaekers, C., (2010) Lacan and organization, , London, London,: MayFlyBooks; Churchman, D., King, S., Academic practice in transition: Hidden stories of academic identities (2009) Teaching in Higher Education, 14 (5), pp. 507-516; Clarke, M., Talkin' 'bout a revolution: The social, political, and fantasmatic logics of education policy (2012) Journal of Education Policy, 27 (2), pp. 173-191; Contu, A., Decaf resistance: On misbehavior, cynicism, and desire in liberal workplaces (2008) Management Communication Quarterly, 21 (3), pp. 364-379; Davies, B., Petersen, E.B., Neo-liberal discourse in the Academy: The forestalling of (collective) resistance (2005) LATISS: Learning and Teaching in the Social Sciences, 2 (2), pp. 77-98; Delanty, G., Academic identities and institutional change (2008) Changing identities in higher education: Voicing perspectives, pp. 124-133. , In: Barnett R., Di Napoli R., editors London, London,: Routledge; Dill, D., The management of academic culture: Notes on the management of meaning and social integration (1982) Higher Education, 11 (3), pp. 303-320; Fletcher, J., Kaufer, K., Shared leadership (2003) Shared leadership, pp. 21-47. , In: Pearce C., Congar J., editors Thousand Oaks, CA, Thousand Oaks, CA,: Sage; Fullan, M., Scott, G., (2009) Turnaround leadership for higher education, , San Francisco, San Francisco,: Jossey-Bass; Glynos, J., Self-transgressive enjoyment as a Freedom Fetter (2008) Political Studies, 56 (3), pp. 679-704; Glynos, J., Lacan at work (2010) Lacan and organization, pp. 13-58. , In: Cederström C., Hoedemaekers C., editors London, London,: MayFlyBooks; Glynos, J., Howarth, D., (2007) Logics of critical explanation in social and political theory, , London, London,: Routledge; Hargreaves, A., Dawe, R., Paths of professional development: Contrived collegiality, collaborative culture, and the case of peer coaching (1990) Teaching and Teacher Education, 6 (3), pp. 227-241; Knight, P., Trowler, P., Department level cultures and the improvement of learning and teaching (2000) Studies in Higher Education, 25 (1), pp. 69-83; Land, R., (2004) Educational development: Discourse, identity and practice, , Maidenhead, Maidenhead,: Open University Press/SRHE; Macfarlane, B., The disengaged academic: The retreat from citizenship (2005) Higher Education Quarterly, 59 (4), pp. 296-312; Macfarlane, B., Defining and rewarding academic citizenship: The implications for university promotions policy (2007) Journal of Higher Education Policy and Management, 29 (3), pp. 261-273; Macfarlane, B., Cheng, M., Communism, universalism and disinterestedness: Re-examining contemporary support among academics for Merton's scientific norms (2008) Journal of Academic Ethics, 6 (1), pp. 67-78; Malcolm, J., Zukas, M., Making a mess of academic work: Experience, purpose and identity (2009) Teaching in Higher Education, 14 (5), pp. 495-506; Marginson, S., Considine, M., (2000) The enterprise university: Power, governance and reinvention in Australia, , Cambridge, Cambridge,: Cambridge University Press; Merton, R., (1996) On social structure and science, , In: Sztompka P., editors Chicago, Chicago,: The University of Chicago Press; Middlehurst, R., Goreham, H., Woodfield, S., Why research leadership in higher education? Exploring contributions from the UK's Leadership Foundation for Higher Education (2009) Leadership, 5 (3), pp. 311-329; Nixon, J., Education for the good society: The integrity of academic practice (2004) London Review of Education, 2 (3), pp. 245-252; Petro, J., Collegiality in history (1992) Bulletin of the New York Academy of Medicine, 68 (2), pp. 286-291; Ramsden, P., (1989) Learning to lead in higher education, , London, London,: RoutledgeFalmer; Rowland, S., Collegiality and intellectual love (2008) British Journal of Sociology of Education, 29 (3), pp. 353-360; Rüegg, W., The academic ethos (1986) Minerva, 24 (4), pp. 393-412; Scott, G., Coates, H., Anderson, M., (2008), http://research.acer.edu.au/higher_education/3, Learning leaders in times of change: Academic leadership capabilities for Australian higher education. Retrieved February 2, 2013, from; Scott, P., Leadership in universities (2011) International Journal of Leadership in Public Services, 7 (3), pp. 229-234; Seigel, M., On collegiality (2004) Journal of Legal Education, 54 (3), pp. 406-441; Shils, E., The academic ethos under strain (1975) Minerva, 13 (1), pp. 1-37; Silver, H., Does a university have a culture? (2003) Studies in Higher Education, 28 (2), pp. 157-169; Smith, J., Forging identities: The experiences of probationary lecturers in the UK (2010) Studies in Higher Education, 35 (5), pp. 577-591; Spicer, A., Cederström, C., For the love of the organization (2010) Lacan and organization, pp. 133-168. , In: Cederström C., Hoedemaekers C., editors London, London,: MayFlyBooks; Spiller, D., Language and academic leadership: Exploring and evaluating the narratives (2010) Higher Education Research & Development, 29 (6), pp. 679-692; Stavrakakis, Y., (1999) Lacan and the political, , London, London,: Routledge; Stavrakakis, Y., Symbolic authority, fantasmatic enjoyment and the spirits of capitalism: Genealogies of mutual engagement (2010) Lacan and organization, pp. 59-100. , In: Cederström C., Hoedemaekers C., editors London, London,: MayFlyBooks; Tannen, D., Agonism in academic discourse (2002) Journal of Pragmatics, 34 (10-11), pp. 1651-1669; Tapper, T., Palfreyman, D., (2010) The collegial tradition in the age of mass higher education, , London, London,: Springer; Taylor, P., Being an academic today (2008) Changing identities in higher education: Voicing perspectives, pp. 27-39. , In: Barnett R., Di Napoli R., editors London, London,: Routledge; Urgo, J., Collegiality and academic community (2007) Symploke, 13 (1-2), pp. 30-42; Waters, M., Collegiality, bureaucratization, and professionalization: A Weberian analysis (1989) American Journal of Sociology, 94 (5), pp. 945-972; Ylijöki, O.H., Academic nostalgia: A narrative approach to academic work (2005) Human Relations, 58 (5), pp. 555-576},
  abstract = {It has been argued that leadership in higher education differs from leadership in other organisational contexts, in part because of the culture of collegiality and autonomy underpinning academic work. Collegiality, however, is a complex and somewhat 'slippery' idea that features in academic leadership literature in a variety of, sometimes contradictory, ways. We argue for the need to unpack and explore the notion of collegiality to understand what makes it such an enduring idea in academic leadership. We start by examining the multiple meanings of collegiality circulating in scholarly writing on leadership to reveal the complexity and tensions it raises for academic leaders. We then turn to Lacanian theory and explore collegiality as a subliminal fantasy that represents an important interface between 'leaders' and those who are 'led', and one which influences our understanding of academic work and leadership. From the perspective offered by our Lacanian exploration, we propose that in order to traverse the nostalgic fantasy of the collegial past, instead of looking for closure, it might be more useful to put indeterminacy, absence and lack at the centre of the academic leadership project. \copyright{} 2014 \copyright{} 2014 HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {academic leadership,collegiality,fantasy,Lacanian theory}
}

@article{klimasImpactInternationalCollaborative2022,
  title = {Impact of the International Collaborative Addiction Medicine Research Fellowship on Physicians' Future Engagement in Addiction Research},
  author = {Klimas, Jan and Dong, Huiru and Hamilton, Michee-Ana and Cullen, Walter and Samet, Jeffrey H. and Wood, Evan and Fairbairn, Nadia},
  year = 2022,
  month = jan,
  journal = {Substance Abuse},
  volume = {43},
  number = {1},
  pages = {809--814},
  issn = {0889-7077},
  doi = {10.1080/08897077.2021.2010256},
  abstract = {Background: To evaluate how an international one-year intensive research training program for addiction medicine physicians contributed to subsequent research involvement and productivity. Methods: We prospectively compared addiction medicine physician fellows admitted to a one-year training program with non-admitted controls, using baseline questionnaire and peer-reviewed publication data. Participants' publication activity was assessed from fellowship application date onwards using biomedical databases (e.g., PubMed, Embase). Results: Between July 2014 and June 2020, which is six years of cohorts, 56 (39 women) physicians, both fellows (n\,=\,25) and non-admitted applicants (n\,=\,31), were observed and included in the study, contributing 261 person-years of observation. At baseline, in the fellows' cohort: 76\% of participants (19/25) reported past research involvement, 24\% (6/25) had one or more advanced graduate degrees (e.g., MPH), and the median number of peer-reviewed, first author publications was one (Interquartile Range [IQR]\,=\,0--2). At baseline, in the controls' cohort: 84\% of participants (26/31) reported past research involvement, 39\% (12/31) had one or more advanced graduate degrees, and the median number of peer-reviewed, first author publications was zero. The physicians' training included internal medicine (n\,=\,8), family medicine (n\,=\,33), psychiatry (n\,=\,5) and others (n\,=\,4). At follow up, there was a significant difference between fellows (n\,=\,25) and controls (n\,=\,31) in total number of publications (Rate Ratio [RR]\,=\,13.09, 95\% Confidence Interval [CI], 5.01\,-\,34.21, p\,{$<$}\,0.001), as well as first author publications (RR = 5.59, 95\% CI, 2.23\,-\,14.06, p\,{$<$}\,0.001). Conclusion: In the six-year observation period, fellows' productivity indicates undertaking this fellowship was associated with significant research outputs in comparison to controls, signaling successful training of addiction physicians to help recruit addiction medicine physicians to participate in addiction research.},
  langid = {english}
}

@article{klineRiseMarketbasedChoice2023,
  title = {The rise of market-based "choice" programs: 100 years of social sorting in social welfare services},
  author = {Kline, {\relax ZD}},
  year = 2023,
  month = dec,
  journal = {SOCIOLOGY COMPASS},
  volume = {17},
  number = {12},
  issn = {1751-9020},
  doi = {10.1111/soc4.13146},
  abstract = {Social service programs in the United States increasingly focus on giving individuals and families more "choices." The approach is exemplified by "choice programs," where people receive services through various forms of market participation. Examples of choice programs include defined contribution retirement plans, housing vouchers, charter schools, and health insurance marketplaces. Individuals across the socioeconomic spectrum have become accustomed to choice programs, and one's social status greatly affects the types, qualities, and prices of services people receive. The importance of the conversation around choice programs for providing access to resources is immense. Yet, despite contentious debate about their utility, we lack a comprehensive understanding of the institutionalization and widespread adoption of choice programs across a diverse range of social service domains. This article synthesizes stratification literature on retirement, healthcare, education, and housing to document the historical rise of choice programs. Particular attention is given to explaining how behavioral patterns and routines become second nature, altering who benefits, who is left out, and the overall effectiveness of the social welfare system.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {AFFORDABLE CARE ACT,choice programs,DESERVING POOR,DIRECTED HEALTH PLANS,DISCRIMINATION,FAMILY,INDIVIDUALIZATION,INSTITUTIONAL ISOMORPHISM,institutions,judgment and decision-making,POLITICS,POVERTY,sciences,social services,social sorting,SOCIOLOGY}
}

@article{kLinkingBehaviorCoauthorship2016,
  title = {Linking behavior in the PER coauthorship network},
  author = {K, Anderson and Matthew, Crespi and Eleanor, C. Sayre},
  year = 2016,
  journal = {arXiv.org},
  abstract = {There is considerable long-term interest in understanding the dynamics of collaboration networks, and how these networks form and evolve over time. Most of the work done on the dynamics of social networks focuses on well-established communities. Work examining emerging social networks is rarer, simply because data is difficult to obtain in real time. In this paper, we use thirty years of data from an emerging scientific community to look at that crucial early stage in the development of a social network. We show that when the field was very young, islands of individual researchers labored in relative isolation, and the coauthorship network was disconnected. Thirty years later, rather than a cluster of individuals, we find a true collaborative community, bound together by a robust collaboration network. However, this change did not take place gradually -- the network remained a loose assortment of isolated individuals until the mid-2000s, when those smaller parts suddenly knit themselves together into a single whole. In the rest of this paper, we consider the role of three factors in these observed structural changes: growth, changes in social norms, and the introduction of institutions such as field-specific conferences and journals. We have data from the very earliest years of the field, a period which includes the introduction of two different institutions: the first field-specific conference, and the first field-specific journals. We also identify two relevant behavioral shifts: a discrete increase in coauthorship coincident with the first conference, and a shift among established authors away from collaborating with outsiders, towards collaborating with each other. The interaction of these factors gives us insight into the formation of collaboration networks more broadly.}
}

