dle River, NJ, Upper Saddle River, NJ,: Merrill Printice Hall; Pintrich, P.R., Smith, D.A.F., Garcia, T., McKeachie, W.J., Reliability and predictive validity of the Motivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire (MSLQ) (1993) Educational and Psychological Measurement, 53 (3), pp. 801-813; Pintrich, P.R., Zusho, A., Student motivation and self-regulated learning in college classroom (2007) The scholarship of teaching and learning in higher education: Evidence-based perspective, pp. 731-810. , In: Perry R. P., Smart J. C., editors New York, New York,: Springer; Pokay, P., Blumenfeld, P.C., Predicting achievement in early and late in the semester: The role of motivation and use of learning strategies (1990) Journal of Educational Psychology, 82 (1), pp. 41-50; Ramsden, P., A performance indicator of teaching quality in higher education: The Course Experience Questionnaire (1991) Studies in Higher Education, 16 (2), pp. 129-150; Richardson, J.T.E., Approaches to studying and perceptions of academic quality in a short web-based course (2003) British Journal of Educational Technology, 34 (4), pp. 433-442; Salili, F., Learning and motivation: An Asian perspective (1996) Psychology in Developing Societies, 8 (1), pp. 55-81; Schunk, D.H., Effects of effort attributional feedback on children's perceived self-efficacy and achievement (1982) Journal of Educational Psychology, 74 (4), pp. 548-556; Schunk, D.H., Sef-efficacy and educational instruction (1995) Self-efficy, adaptation and adjustment: Theory, research and application, pp. 281-303. , In: Maddux J. E., editors New York: Plenum Press; Schunk, D.H., Pintrich, P.R., Meece, J.L., (2008) Motivation in education: Theory, research and applications, , 3, Englewood Cliffs, NJ, Englewood Cliffs, NJ,: Prentice Hall; Skinner, E., Zimmere-Gembeck, M.J., Connel, J.P., Individual differences and the development of perceived control (1998) Monographs of the Society for Research in Child Development, 63 (2-3), pp. 1-234; Tinto, V., (1987) Leaving college: Rethinking the causes and cures of student attrition, , Chicago, IL, Chicago, IL,: The University of Chicago Press; Trigwell, K., Prosser, M., Improving the quality of learning: The influence of learning context and student approaches to learning on learning outcomes (1991) Higher Education, 22 (3), pp. 251-266; (1998) World declaration on higher education for the twenty-first century:Vision and action and framework for priority action for change and development in higher education adopted by the World Conference on Higher Education Higher Education in the Twenty-First Century: Vision and action, , UNESCO, Paris,: Author; Watson, M., McSorley, M., Foxcroft, C., Watson, A., Exploring the motivational orientation and learning strategies of first year university learners (2004) Tertiary Education and Management, 10 (3), pp. 193-207; Wigfield, A., Eccles, J.S., Expectancy-value theory of achievement motivation (2000) Contemporary Educational Psychology, 25 (1), pp. 68-81; Wilson, K.L., Lizzio, A., Ramsden, P., The development, validation and application of the Course Experience Questionnaire (1997) Studies in Higher Education, 22 (1), pp. 33-53},
  abstract = {While there is considerable literature concerning the relationship between student motivation and achievement, few studies have examined the relationship between student motivation, course experiences and academic achievement at the postgraduate level. This paper is based on the findings of the initial correlation analysis of the relationship between motivational beliefs and course experiences of 368 postgraduate students in five different subject areas enrolled in day and afternoon classes at a highly-ranked public university in Pakistan. A questionnaire was developed using scales from the Motivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire and the Course Experiences Questionnaire to measure the motivational beliefs and course experiences of students. Academic achievement was measured using the achievement score in the courses in which the data were collected. The results of the study indicated that students' achievement scores were positively correlated with their self-efficacy for learning and negatively correlated with test anxiety, whereas the course-experience factor learning community was significantly correlated with achievement scores. Significant correlations were also found among almost all motivational beliefs and course experience factors. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {academic achievement,course experiences,motivational beliefs}
}

