 159-174. , doi:10.1002/(SICI)1099-1379(199903)20:2&159::AID-JOB881>3.0.CO;2-7; Santos, G.G., Cabral-Cardoso, C., WF culture in academia: A gendered view of work/family conflict and coping strategies (2008) Gender in Management - An International Journal, 23 (6), pp. 442-457. , doi:10.1108/17542410810897553; Schieman, S., Glavin, P., Trouble at the border? Gender, flexible work conditions and the work-home interface (2008) Social Problems, 55 (4), pp. 590-611. , doi:10.1525/sp.2008.55.4.590; Schieman, S., Young, M., The demands of creative work: Implications for stress in the work/family interface (2010) Social Science Research, 39 (2), pp. 246-259. , doi:10.1016/j.ssresearch.2009.05.008; Schor, N., The supportive academic environment: Ingredients for success (2003) Pediatric Neurology, 29 (5), pp. 370-373. , doi:10.1016/S0887-8994(03)00308-4; Sumer, H.C., Knight, P.A., How do people with different attachment styles balance work with family? A personality perspective on WF linkage (2001) Journal of Applied Psychology, 86 (4), pp. 653-663. , doi:10.1037/0021-9010.86.4.653; Vigoda, E., The relationship between organizational politics, job attitudes and work outcomes: Exploration and implications for the public sector (2000) Journal of Vocational Behavior, 57 (3), pp. 326-347. , doi:10.1006/jvbe.1999.1742; Vigoda, E., Internal politics in public administration systems: An empirical examination of its relationship with job congruence, organizational citizenship behavior and in-role performances (2000) Public Personnel Management, 29 (2), pp. 185-210; Voydanoff, P., Work and family: A review and expanded conceptualization (1988) Journal of Social Behavior & Personality, 3 (4), pp. 1-22; Voydanoff, P., (2007) Work, family and community: Exploring interconnections, , Mahwah, NJ, Mahwah, NJ,: Routledge; Wiersma, U.J., Gender differences in job attitude preferences: Work home role conflict and job level as mediating variables (1990) Journal of Occupational Psychology, 63 (3), pp. 231-243. , doi:10.1111/j.2044-8325.1990.tb00524.x; Williams, K.J., Alliger, G.M., Role stressors, mood spillover and perceptions of work/family conflict in employed parents (1994) Academy of Management Journal, 37 (4), pp. 837-868. , doi:10.2307/256602; Woodward, D., Work life balancing strategies used by women managers in British 'modern' universities (2007) Equal Opportunities International, 26 (1), pp. 6-17. , doi:10.1108/02610150710726507},
  abstract = {The higher education landscape is undergoing major transformation, with a significant impact on the work and family practices of academics and professional staff. The purpose of this exploratory study is to examine the extent to which (1) time-related, (2) strain-related and (3) demographical variables impact on the work/family balance of academic and professional staff in Victorian universities and Technical and Further Education (TAFE) institutes. Our findings reveal that university employees experience greater work/family imbalance. The results of this research demonstrate how the three constructs contribute to work/family imbalance in academia, especially within the university sector. This paper is believed to be the first to explore work/family balance from an Australian cross-sectoral perspective. It provides an agenda for future theory and research to increase understanding of work/family balance from a cross-sectoral perspective. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
