 First Aid, Retrieved May 25, 2011, from; Palmer, S., Puri, A., (2006) Coping with stress at university: A survival guide, , London, London,: Sage; Richards, D., Tangney, B., An informal online learning community for student mental health at university: A preliminary investigation (2008) British Journal of Guidance & Counselling, 36 (1), pp. 81-97. , doi:10.1080/03069880701715671; Rickwood, D., Deane, F.P., Wilson, C.J., Ciarrochi, J., Young people's help-seeking for mental health problems (2005) Australian e-Journal for the Advancement of Mental Health, 4 (SUPPL. 3), pp. 1-34; Rickwood, D.J., Deane, F.P., Wilson, C.J., When and how do young people seek professional help for mental health problems (2007) Medical Journal of Australia, 187 (7), pp. S35-S39; Schwartz, S., (2008) Don't fail your friends. The Australian: Higher Education Section, , http://www.vc.mq.edu.au/press-coverage/press-coverage610.pdf, February 20, Retrieved June 11, 2009, from; (2002) Education of students with disabilities, , Senate Employment Workplace Relations and Education References Committee, Canberra, Canberra,: Commonwealth of Australia; Stallman, H.M., Prevalence of psychological distress in university students: Implications for service delivery (2008) Australian Family Physician, 37 (8), pp. 673-677; Stallman, H.M., Embedding resilience within the tertiary curriculum: A feasibility study (2011) Higher Education Research and Development, 30 (2), pp. 121-134; Stallman, H.M., Shochet, I., Prevalence of mental health problems in Australian university health services (2009) Australian Psychologist, 44 (2), pp. 122-127. , doi:10.1080/00050060902733727; (2008) Command syntax reference, , SPSS®, (Version 17.0) [Computer software]. Chicago, IL: SPSS Inc; (2006) Developing a stigma-reduction initiative (SAMHSA PubNo SMA-4176), , Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration, Rockville, MD, Rockville, MD,: Centre for Mental Health Services, Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration; Turner, A., Hammond, C., Gilchrist, M., Barlow, J., Coventry university students' experiences of mental health problems (2007) Counselling Psychology Quarterly, 20 (3), pp. 247-252. , doi:10.1080/09515070701570451; (2001) The World Health Report 2001: Mental health - new understanding, new hope, , World Health Organisation, Geneva, Geneva,: Author; Wilhelm, K.A., Finch, A.W., Davenport, T.A., Hickie, I.B., What can alert the general practitioner to people whose common mental health problems are unrecognised? (2008) Medical Journal of Australia, 188 (12 SUPPL.), pp. S114-S118; Wright, A., Jorm, A.F., Mackinnon, A.J., Labelling of mental disorders and stigma in young people (2011) Social Science & Medicine, 73 (4), pp. 498-506. , doi: 10.1016/j.socscimed.2011.06.015; Wynaden, D., Chapman, R., Orb, A., McGowan, S., Yeak, S., Zeeman, Z., Factors that influence people from Asian communities' access to and delivery of mental health care (2005) International Journal of Mental Health Nursing, 14 (2), pp. 88-95; Yap, M.B.H., Wright, A., Jorm, A., The influence of stigma on young people's help-seeking intentions and beliefs about the helpfulness of various sources of help (2011) Social Psychiatry and Psychiatric Epidemiology, 46, pp. 1257-1265},
  abstract = {Within the university community, student demographic data highlights a high level of cultural diversity and an 'at-risk' population for the onset of mental health problems. Moreover, the transition to higher education is itself associated with a range of emotional, social and academic demands that can cause psychological distress. Therefore, at any one time, a substantial number of students may be attempting to complete university studies while managing problematic symptoms, behaviours or an emerging or diagnosed mental disorder. The aim of this paper is to provide a snapshot of these students' experiences to enable strategic planning of future university support services. Of particular importance was the identification of facilitators and barriers that this group of students faced while engaged in university life. Participants were 1378 students enrolled at a Western Australian university who accessed a text-based online survey during May 2009. The students provided descriptions of the range of problematic symptoms, behaviours and/or disorders that were causing interference with their lives, and university career. Not surprisingly, the most commonly identified disorders with the participant population were depression, anxiety and stress-related concerns. More than half the sample had not sought professional help. The results highlight the fact that universities are well placed to play a major role in improving pathways to primary care and early intervention for students with an emerging or diagnosed mental health problem and to identify and support students trying to manage the 'normal' psychological demands associated with higher education. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {higher education,mental health problems,mental illness,psychological stress,student support}
}

